  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDry conditions are expected for the next 24 hours ahead of a more\nactive pattern starting Wednesday evening. This afternoon, a dry \ncold front has been working through northern Ohio, and is roughly in \nline with the I-70 corridor as of this writing. Light northwest \nwinds are observed in west-central and central Ohio with\nsouthwesterly winds observed in eastern Indiana and southwest Ohio. \n1\nThe front will stall later this evening, allowing winds to become\nlight and variable. Widespread fog is not currently expected, but the\nstalled front and clear skies do support the potential for radiation\nfog late tonight into Wednesday morning. The dry conditions\nassociated with the front are a limiting factor and likely prevent\nthis from being a widespread issue. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs the low pressure approaches the area tomorrow, clouds will\nincrease in coverage, especially into the late afternoon and evening.\nA band of showers and perhaps a few rumbles of thunder move into\nsouthwest portions of the area during the evening hours. Ahead of\nthis band, a few showers may pop up along the stalled front, which is\nstill expected to be along and north of the I-70 corridor. Forecast\nsoundings show a very narrow CAPE area for only a few hours across\nportions of southwest Indiana and western Ohio so thunder mentions\nare very limited within the forecast. Due to the later timing, the\nsevere threat appears lower at this time. If the timing moves closer\nto the early evening, a few strong wind gusts would be possible in\nsoutheastern Indiana (SPC Day 2 Marginal Risk). \n\nOtherwise, slow moving showers along the front combined with the band\nof rain provide some potential local rainfall amounts to be between\n0.50-1\". Most locations see less than 0.50\". \n\nWednesday night, the low pressure deepens as it shifts east out of \nthe region. While not during an optimal diurnal window, the pressure \nresponse on the backside of the low pressure may result in a period \nof gusty winds overnight into early Thursday morning. Most gusts \nshould peak in the 30 mph range, but a few gusts up to 35 mph are \npossible. \n\nFrom Thursday through Saturday, deep cyclonic flow is persistent over\nthe region. Expect below normal temperatures, widespread cloud \ncoverage, and northwesterly flow. Precipitation chances are highest\non Thursday, and there may be enough cold air to produce the first\nflakes of the season. \n\nTemperatures gradually begin to climb back into the 50s early next\nweek, remaining a few degrees below normal.",
